Authorize the media endpoints by playback ticket, not by subnet

Eleven media-serving actions in v10.11.11 carry no [Authorize] attribute. Only
DefaultPolicy is configured in ApiServiceCollectionExtensions -- there is no
FallbackPolicy and no global AuthorizeFilter -- so an action without an explicit
attribute carries no authorization metadata and is served anonymously.

The item id is not a secret either. LibraryManager.GetNewItemIdInternal derives it as
MD5(type.FullName + path) over UTF-16LE bytes, so it is a pure function of the file
path. Verified end to end on this server: hashing a path alone reproduced the id the
server uses, and fetching it with no token returned 206 and the media body, while
GET /Items/{id} for the same item returned 401.

WHY A BARE [Authorize] DOES NOT WORK, measured rather than assumed

An earlier revision of this patch used [Authorize] and broke direct play. Jellyfin for
Android TV 0.19.10 composes the direct play URL itself and attaches no credential.
From this server's own log, 2026-09-02 00:21:

    GET /Videos/{id}/stream?container=mkv&static=true&tag=...&mediaSourceId=...
        &streamOptions=%7B%7D&enableAudioVbrEncoding=true   -> 401

No ApiKey, no api_key, no PlaySessionId, no auth header. The client reported "Player
error encountered, will retry", re-POSTed PlaybackInfo and fell back to
/Videos/{id}/hls1/main/*.ts -- a server-side remux on every single start. Upstream
knows: HlsSegmentController carries the comment "Can't require authentication just yet
due to seeing some requests come from Chrome without full query string".

This also settles a question an earlier write-up left open. Server-minted signed URLs
would NOT fix this client, because it does not use the URL the server hands it.
Contrast DynamicHlsController, which is [Authorize] at class level and works fine,
because there the server writes the segment URLs -- token included -- into the
playlist it returns.

WHY NOT AnonymousLanAccessPolicy

The revision before this one used it. It was honest but inert: every path that can
reach this server (LAN, Docker bridge, Tailnet) is already inside
NetworkConfiguration.LocalNetworkSubnets, so it refused no request that stock Jellyfin
would have served, and its deny branch could not even be exercised on this host -- a
container on an excluded bridge still arrives NATed as an address that is on the list.

THE APPROACH

An authenticated playback negotiation grants access to the media it just handed out.

MediaInfoController is [Authorize], so every PlaybackInfo call has a user behind it.
MediaInfoHelper.GetPlaybackInfo now issues a ticket keyed on the caller's normalized
remote address, covering the item id and every media source id in the response.
StreamAccessHandler redeems it for anonymous requests, matching the item id, the media
source id (route or query -- for a multi-version item they differ) and the subtitle and
attachment route ids. Each use slides the expiry so a long or paused playback does not
expire mid-stream. Tickets live 4 hours and only in memory; a restart tears down the
streams anyway, and the next play re-negotiates.

An authenticated caller passes as before. StreamAccessRequirement derives from
DefaultAuthorizationRequirement so DefaultAuthorizationHandler still applies the
remote-access permission and parental-schedule checks; it leaves success to the
subclass, and its Fail() is final either way.

Net effect: a caller that never authenticated gets nothing, on any subnet. Guessing a
file path is no longer enough, which is the actual defect.

Residual, stated plainly: a ticket is bound to an address, so anything sharing that
address -- another process on the same host, or a second device behind the same NAT --
can ride a ticket while a legitimate playback is live. That is strictly narrower than
"anyone who can reach the port, at any time, for any item".

SCOPE NOTES

The two LiveTv routes keep AnonymousLanAccessPolicy. They are keyed by a
server-generated recording/stream id rather than an item id, so no ticket covers them,
and they are consumed by ffmpeg on the server itself via GetApiUrlForLocalAccess().
Unlike item ids, those ids are not derived from a file path, so they are not guessable.

Registration lives in ApplicationHost rather than Jellyfin.Server's
AddJellyfinApiAuthorization, because AuthorizationOptions composes across Configure
delegates and this keeps every change inside the four assemblies the local build
already replaces. The policy name is a constant on the requirement rather than on
Policies for the same reason: MediaBrowser.Common is not one of them.

Tests cover the branch the LAN policy could not: anonymous with no ticket is refused,
a ticket does not travel to another address or cover another item, a query-string
media source id is honoured, and an authenticated caller needs no ticket.

